<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n36270" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">Pīĕrĭa</span></b> ,
		ae, f., = <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">Πιερία</span>. <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b>
			<i>A country of Macedonia</i>, <i>south
				of the Haliacmon</i>, <i><span class="en">Mel. 2, 3, 2</span></i>; <i><span class="en">Plin. 4, 10,
				17, § 33</span></i>; <i><span class="en">Liv. 39,
			26</span></i>.— Hence, </div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense2"><b>B.</b>
			<b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">Pīĕrĭcus</span></b> , a,
			um, adj., <i>Pierian</i>: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">pix,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Plin.
					14, 20, 25, § 128</span></i>
			.—</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>II.</b>
			<i>A country of Syria</i>, <i>between
				Cilicia and Phœnicia</i>, <i>with a
				mountain of the same name</i>, <i>and the capital
				city</i> Seleucia Pieria, <i><span class="en">Cic. Att.
				11, 20, 1</span></i>; <i><span class="en">Plin. 5, 12, 13, § 67</span></i>;
				<i><span class="en">5, 21, 18, §
			79</span></i>.</div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
    <div class="alpheios-lex-alph-source">From <u>
            <u>A Latin Dictionary</u>
        </u> (Charlton T. Lewis, Charles Short, Oxford, Clarendon Press, 1879) </div>
</div>